From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Give SP and DP edges priority, and reserve queue slots

The scheduler's interrupt queue holds eight messages and serves three things at
once: our retrace edges, our SP and DP edges, and -- through the command queue
that __scHandleRetrace drains -- the game's own task submissions. One retrace too
many takes the place of a task completion, and of a task start.

Measured on Windows 95: the RSP completion was refused, requeued, and deposited
only nine refusals later. The game does not wait that long; a few frames without
an answer and its scheduler gives up on the task and clears curRSPTask. Our
message then arrives and __scHandleRSP dereferences a null pointer, the
assert() that would have caught it having been compiled out.

Two changes, and the second only became possible after the first attempt failed.

**Priority.** On hardware an SP interrupt and a vertical retrace are independent
events whose relative order is not guaranteed, so serving SP and DP first is
faithful. Their order among themselves is preserved, which is the one the game
observes.

**Reserved slots.** A first attempt refused to deposit a retrace while the
previous one was still undelivered. It stopped the game from booting at all: our
external queue is not drained on a schedule but opportunistically, when a guest
thread happens to wait, so the flag stayed set and every later retrace was
discarded. Any policy that assumes periodic draining is wrong here by
construction. This version looks at the guest queue's actual state at deposit
time and keeps nothing between passes. On hardware a retrace raised while the
queue is full is lost the same way -- osSendMesg is called without blocking from
the interrupt.

Measured before and after:

    before   1 task submitted, 1 SP edge, 0 display lists
    after   173 tasks, 1274 SP edges, 545 DP edges, 550 display lists,
            audio voices normalised, 3540 frames presented, and no message
            refused at all

The game now runs its audio engine and submits display lists. The remaining crash
is further along, in __scHandleRDP, and it is no longer starvation: nothing is
refused any more.

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
